The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (WARN_ON(!ctrl_ptp) || pf->hw.mac_type == ICE_MAC_UNKNOWN)
    return -ENODEV;
Reason:
In VFIO passthrough setups where only a PF without the source timer is passed, the controlling PF (adapter->ctrl_pf) is never initialized in the VM, so ice_get_ctrl_ptp() returns NULL. The WARN_ON macro then triggers an unnecessary kernel warning, even though this behavior is expected in that configuration.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!ctrl_ptp) {
    dev_info(ice_pf_to_dev(pf), "PTP unavailable: no controlling PF\n");
    return -EOPNOTSUPP;
}
if (pf->hw.mac_type == ICE_MAC_UNKNOWN)
    return -ENODEV;
Reason:
Instead of using WARN_ON, the fix first checks if ctrl_ptp is NULL and returns -EOPNOTSUPP with an informational message. This gracefully handles the expected scenario where the controlling PF is unavailable, without triggering a kernel warning. The separate check for ICE_MAC_UNKNOWN remains to handle actual hardware errors.

